course: dictionary
lesson: 3276
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can mean different things in different subjects. Erratic is an adjective that means not regular or steady, and it changes suddenly in a way you cannot predict. «erratic behaviour» is behaviour that jumps about, so you never know what will happen next.
explain: In science and data, erratic results jump around and are hard to trust. In geography, the word can also be a noun, and it is then a special name for a rock. «Glaciers dropped huge boulders, called erratics, far from the rocks they came from.» Here an erratic is a boulder that a glacier carried far away from its home.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping it into the sentence. In «Glaciers dropped huge boulders, called erratics», the word erratics names the boulders, so it must mean boulders carried by ice. The clue is the word glaciers, which tells you what kind of boulder it is.
fact: Erratic means not regular or steady, and it can change suddenly.
fact: «Erratic behaviour» means behaviour you cannot predict, which can worry people.
fact: In geology, an erratic is a large boulder carried far by a glacier and left where that type of rock is not normally found.
fact: Glacial erratics show where ice once moved.
fact: Scientists say erratic results are hard to trust because they jump around and do not follow a pattern.
exercise: «Glaciers dropped huge boulders, called erratics, far from the rocks they came from.» What is an erratic here? | A sudden storm | A rock found in the same place it began | *A huge boulder carried far by a glacier | A kind of ice cube
exercise: A driver makes «erratic driving» choices, speeding up and slowing down with no warning. What does erratic suggest? | Very slow and careful | *Unpredictable and unsafe for other people | Funny and silly | Steady and calm, always the same speed
exercise: Find and copy the word in «Glaciers dropped huge boulders, called erratics, far from the rocks they came from.» that names the boulders as a special kind. | *erratics | glaciers | far | dropped
test: A scientist says some results are erratic. What does she mean? | The results are very big and round | Every result is exactly the same | *The results jump around and are hard to trust | The results were written in red
test: A teacher says a child's mood is erratic. What does she mean? | They are very tall for their age | They like the colour red | *Their mood changes suddenly and cannot be predicted | Their mood is calm and steady every day
exam: Which sentence uses erratic as a geology noun? | «The erratic driver swerved across the road.» | «Her erratic singing made everyone laugh.» | «The erratic weather changed every hour.» | *«The erratic sat in the field, carried there by an old glacier.»
